# A: alloc(cells) -> addr.  W9-3C/M-MEM2: a size-classed free list in front of
# the bump allocator of W6/M-MEM (see the "W6 allocator" block near the bottom).
# Every block now carries a HEADER cell in front of the address A returns:
#     [cap][payload: cap cells]      A returns the payload address, header at -8
# cap is the block's capacity in cells, which is the request ROUNDED UP to its
# size class: class k (0..6) holds exactly 4<<k cells (4, 8, … 256); class 7
# holds everything larger, at max(c, 512) cells.  The class of c is a straight
# line of seven unsigned compares — k = #{t in 4 8 16 32 64 128 256 : c >u t} —
# so A stays loop-free.  Head of class k: the cell 0x4F00C0 + 8k (5177536 +
# 8k, REGISTRY §3).  A pops the head when it is non-zero AND its capacity is >=
# c (always true in classes 0..6; class 7 checks only its head, first-fit-at-
# head, never walks); otherwise it bumps as before, with the header in front.
# A freed block's header holds -cap (⌫'s double-free mark), so the fit test
# negates it and a pop writes +cap back.  s5 holds the class cell, then the
# popped block or the new block's base — never across a call (\brk is an
# atom), so A still needs no spill frame.  A is an ALLOCATOR, not a list word:
# it returns a RAW address (no tag), because the tool libraries hand its blocks
# to syscalls; the header is invisible to every caller, which only ever used
# the payload.
# W11-2D: a freed block holds the POISON -559038737 in payload cell 0 (its
# length cell) and the free-list link in payload cell 1 (every class has >= 4
# cells, so cell 1 always exists).  A pop reads the link from cell 1 and writes
# 0 over the poison, so a reused block is never mistaken for a freed one, even
# by a caller that fills the length last.  Both paths count the block into
# ‹heapstats›'s cells: 5180096 live blocks += 1, 5180104 live bytes += the
# whole block, header included, (cap+1)*8 (the bump path has it in s5 already).
