# --- W2-TAG words ---
# M-TAG — shape polymorphism.  Since M-TAG2 (W9-2A) a GOLF value is a LIST iff
# its top 16 bits are exactly 0x7FFF (W11-1A; 0x0001 before) — a heap address
# tagged with 0x7FFF<<48, which is
# what every list word returns; everything else is an INT, including the -1/0
# flags \eq/\rlt produce (top bits 0xFFFF), every negative number and every
# value below 0x7FFF<<48 — in particular a raw heap address.  The integers
# sacrificed are [0x7FFF<<48, 2^63), the 2^48 just below INT64_MAX.  Until M-TAG2
# this was a RANGE test against the heap-bounds cells, which classified an integer that happened to equal a heap address as a
# list; the cells are now A's alone.
# Top-level init: the prelude is prepended source, so plain tokens run at program
# start, before any user code.  W6/M-MEM moved that init to the "W6 allocator"
# block below, where it asks the kernel for the bounds instead of naming them —
# both cells are still written before anything can allocate or call T, because
# every allocation happens in a word body and user code runs last.
# T  v -> 0 (list) / 1 (int).  Zero-is-true, so `T[…]` runs the LIST path.
# v>>48 (logical) is 32767 exactly for a tagged list (a negative gives 65535); \eq makes that -1/0 and 1\radd
# maps it to 0/1.  A leaf: no scratch, hence no spill frame, hence callable anywhere
# — including from inside D's own dispatch, where scratch is live.
