# --- W4 cells: list cells are 8 bytes (M4, shipped by W4A) ---
# The widening is library-only — no compiler change, no template change, so the
# seed and the fixpoint are untouched.  Three rules cover the whole prelude:
#   * every list cell (the length header included) is \fetch / \store, and every
#     element/index step is 8\rmul / 8\radd, so l[i] is at l + 8 + 8*i;
#   * every scratch slot is \fetch / \store against the 8-byte-stride bank at
#     0x4F0060 (X/Y therefore spill 64-byte frames);
#   * the FIXED cells — heap pointer, spill pointer, heap base/span, code arena,
#     the M-VEC hook cells, and a string's 4-byte length prefix — keep 32-bit
#     `@`/`!`, because their addresses and strides are hard-coded elsewhere.
# --- W6 allocator (M-MEM): the list heap is brk-grown ---
# Through W5 the heap was a fixed hole in the BSS: base 0x500000, span up to the
# return-stack top 0xC00000.  Seven megabytes, hard-coded in two stores, and the
# 917,504th cell of a list ran into the return stack and took the program with
# it.  W6 hands the job to the kernel: tools/mkblob2.py shrinks v2's p_memsz so
# the BSS ends at 0x600000 (image + banks + return stack, nothing more), and
# everything above that is the brk heap, grown on demand by the ⌸ atom (0xA6).
#
#   0⌸       -> the current program break (the heap base)
#   addr⌸    -> move the break there; returns the break IN EFFECT afterwards
#
# The base is NOT 0x600000 and must never be written down: the kernel ASLR-shifts
# the initial break by up to 32 MB.  That is the point of the two bounds cells —
# 0x4F0034 base and 0x4F0038 span — which W2 introduced precisely so W6 could
# fill them from the kernel.  Through W8 `T` and the M-VEC templates read them
# too (a value was a list iff (v - base) <u span); since M-TAG2 a list carries
# its own tag and the two cells are A's alone: base + H is the next block, and
# H passing span is what makes A move the break.
#
# Span is taken from what brk RETURNS rather than what it was asked for, so a
# refusal cannot leave the cell claiming memory the process does not own.
# The standing assumption is that the break stays below 2^32: both cells are
# 4 bytes (only A reads them; widening them would be a prelude-only change).
# `@`/`!` zero-extend, so the full unsigned 32-bit range round-trips exactly;
# observed breaks here run from ~0x1C00000 to ~0x3B000000, three orders of
# magnitude of headroom.  What would NOT survive is a >= 4 GB break, which
# needs the two cells (and H) widened first.
# Stack-neutral; 32-bit `!` is right for both cells (each is < 2^31 and BSS
# leaves the high half zero).  1 MB is the initial chunk and also A's growth
# increment: A extends to (needed + 1 MB), so one oversized allocation still
# takes exactly one syscall.
