# === W11-1C buffered output ===
# Ṅ ⍕ ␤ 🗲 ⊐ (N Q E O J) append their bytes to an 8 KB buffer instead of making
# one write(2) per byte (1000000⍳⍕ went from 6.9 million writes to ~840).
# REGISTRY §3: the buffer is 0x430000..0x431FFF (4390912), the pending-byte
# count the cell 0x4F0980 (5179776), the fd it drains to 0x4F0988 (5179784;
# 0 = fd 1), and 0x4F0990 (5179792) the FLUSH HOOK: the address of ‹flush›,
# stored by ‹_1c_init› below.  Every template that exits or does raw I/O — `.`,
# the end of the program, `)`, `(`, the divide-by-zero exit — calls through
# that cell first when it is non-zero (tools/mkblob2._FLUSH_CALL), so nothing
# buffered is lost at exit and a raw `)` byte or `(` read never overtakes it.
# ‹throw›, X's spill-stack error and ‹getc›'s refill call ‹flush› themselves.
# Not flushed: a death by signal (the return-stack guard page's SIGSEGV, a
# kill) loses whatever is still pending, at most 8 KB.
#   ‹flush›      ->     write out the pending bytes now
#   ‹_1c_put›    c ->   append byte c; drain when the buffer is full
# Both are LEAVES: no X frame, no s0..s7, only the 1C cells (5179800 holds the
# drain offset), so ‹throw›, X and ‹getc› may call ‹flush› and N may recurse
# through ‹_1c_put›.  ‹flush› loops on short writes; a write that fails (<= 0)
# drops the rest of the buffer rather than spin.
:‹flush›5179776\fetch0\eq[0 5179800\store
