# === W11-2B dict and set ===
# Hash dict and set, keyed by ints or (nested) lists, compared with ≣.
#   ‹hash›  v -> h        FNV-1a 64-bit.  An int: its 8 bytes, low byte first.
#                         A list: from the FNV basis, each element's 8 bytes in
#                         turn, an inner list standing as the 8 bytes of ITS
#                         ‹hash› — so ≣-equal values hash equal (and [5] hashes
#                         like 5; ≣ tells them apart)
#   ‹dict›  -> d          a new empty dict
#   ‹put›   d k v -> d    set k to v (insert, or overwrite in place)
#   ‹get›   d k -> v      the value; a missing k throws `key not found`
#   ‹has›   d k -> f      0 if k is present, else 1 (zero-is-true, like ∊)
#   ‹del›   d k -> d      remove k (no-op when absent)
#   ‹keys›  d -> list     the live keys, in insertion order (a key deleted and
#                         put again goes to the end)
#   ‹vals›  d -> list     their values, in the same order
#   ‹set›   -> s          a dict whose values are all 0;  ‹add›  s k -> s
# d is UPDATED IN PLACE: ‹put› ‹del› ‹add› return the same d, so `‹put›_` is
# fine.  A key list is stored by reference (not copied): changing or freeing
# it while it is a key breaks the dict.  d is a tagged 6-cell list (≢d is 6,
# not the count — use ‹keys›≢):
#   d[0] index table (raw A block, cap 4-byte slots)
#   d[1] cap (a power of two, >= 8)             d[2] 64 - log2(cap) (the shift)
#   d[3] entries (raw A block, cap/2 triples)   d[4] used (entries appended)
#   d[5] live (the count)
# A CPython-style compact dict: entries are appended in insertion order as
# triples [D key value] (D >= 0 the key's stored hash, see ‹_2b_find›; -1
# marks a deleted entry), and each 4-byte index slot holds an entry's address
# (0 = empty), found by linear probing from D's home slot.  (W11-2B stored
# h' = ‹hash›>>1 and entry number + 1 in 8-byte slots; W12-P2 changed the
# int-key hash, the slot width and the slot contents, nothing a program sees:
# ‹hash› itself is unchanged.)  A deleted entry's slot stays as a tombstone.
# When used reaches cap/2 the entries are compacted into new blocks — at
# 2*cap when live >= cap/4 (4*cap once that reaches 131072), else at the same
# cap — the index is rebuilt and the old blocks go back via ⌫, so the
# table's load (tombstones included) never passes 1/2.
# 2B cells: 5179968 ‹_2b_h8›'s byte source · 5179976 / 5179984 / 5179992
# short-lived temps (no call in between) · 5180008 ‹_2b_zero›'s end.  The
# W12-P2 cells (5180736…5180792) are listed in that section.
# Helpers: ‹_2b_h8› (h -> h', eight FNV-1a rounds over the int in 5179968),
# ‹_2b_zero› (addr n ->, zero n cells, n a multiple of 4), ‹_2b_find› (d k ->
# e: the entry's address or 0), ‹_2b_grow› (d ->, compact/grow), ‹_2b_col›
# (d off -> list, off 8 keys / 16 values); W12-P2's ‹_12p2_ins› and
