# ‹_2b_find›  d k -> e   (rewritten by W12-P2: one probe, no X frame).  e is
# the address of k's entry [D key value], or 0 when k is absent.  It leaves
# its state in the P2 cells for the caller: 5180736 d (masked) · 5180744 k ·
# 5180752 D · 5180760 (on a miss) the empty slot the probe stopped at, where
# an insert goes · 5180776 the index table · 5180784 cap-1.  5180792 holds
# the multiplier 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15, stored by ‹dict›; 5180768 is the
# callers' value cell; 5179984 holds a hit for a moment.  A leaf for an int
# key; a list key calls ‹hash› and ≣, which keep out of these cells.  The
# slot walks on the data stack.
# D, the stored hash, is >= 0 (-1 marks a deleted entry), and the home slot
# is ((D >> (shift+2)) << 3) | (D & 7): the top log2(cap)-3 bits of D pick a
# group of eight slots and D's low three bits the slot in it.
#   int k:  x = k>>3, x ^= x>>11, M = x*0x9E3779B97F4A7C15,
#           D = ((M>>1) & ~7) | (k&7) — so 8 consecutive ints share a
#           group (one cache line of the index): sequential keys probe
#           sequential memory, and the xor-shift keeps power-of-two strides
#           from piling up.  The probe compares the raw key cells with ≡ and
#           never calls ≣: a list key is tagged, so it never equals an
#           untagged int, and ‹del› writes the tagged null 0x7FFF<<48 over a
#           deleted entry's key.
#   list k: D = (‹hash› * 0x9E3779B97F4A7C15) >> 1; an entry matches when its
#           D equals and its key ≣ k (≣ only on a D match).
# Either way the probe stops at an empty slot (0) or a hit; a miss steps the
# slot mod cap.  The table is at most half full, so an empty slot exists.
