# === W11-3A catch ===
# ‹catch›  … ′f -> … results 0   |   … msg      W11-3A: run f; catch a throw.
# A handler RECORD is one 64-byte spill-stack frame (pushed by X, so it costs
# no new memory): [h] sp · [h+8] bp · [h+16] the previous chain head; the chain
# head 0x4F0B00 (5180160) holds the newest record's address, 0 when none is
# armed.  The record's own offset IS the spill-stack depth to restore, so it is
# not stored.  Steps:
#   1. X — save the caller's scratch s0..s7 (a throw out of f leaves them as
#      whatever word was running clobbered them; the throw puts them back).
#   2. Park f in s3 (saved already), then ⊷: sp is the data stack as ‹catch›
#      was called MINUS f, bp is ‹catch›'s OWN frame, whose [rbp] — the
#      return address into ‹catch›'s caller — stays live for as long as f
#      runs.  (Capturing bp in a helper word that has already returned would
#      not do: its return-stack slot is reused by the very next call, f's.)
#   3. Stage sp bp and the old head in s0..s2, push them as the record frame
#      with X, link it as the new head, ⍎ f (back from s3).
#   4. f returns: unlink the record (head := [h+16], offset -= 64), Y, push 0
#      above f's results.
#   5. A ‹throw› anywhere below (in f, a € / ⇤ / ⊡ word, a nested ‹catch›
#      that re-throws) parks the message, ‹_3a_pop›s the record (spill offset
#      back to it, so ‹catch›'s scratch frame is on top), Y (the caller's
#      scratch back), ⊶, and pushes the message: the throwing word's epilogue
#      then returns through ‹catch›'s [rbp], so ‹catch› returns to its caller
#      with … msg.  The data f pushed or consumed is discarded — the stack is
#      back at the depth of the ‹catch› call, f dropped; the return stack is
#      ‹catch›'s caller's (any ⊡ frames in between are dropped with it).  The
#      message is a tagged code list, never 0.
# Not restored: global variables (→x), the heap (nothing f allocated is
# freed), ∘/⊸ thunks (heap blocks, leaked like the rest), buffered output (what f printed stays printed,
# in order).  Nesting needs nothing more: each record links the one before,
# and a handler that calls ‹throw› again reaches the next one out.
:‹catch›X5177464\store\spr5177448\store5177440\store5180160\fetch5177456\store X
