# === W12-A6 fixpoint ===
# ⍣ (0xD9)  x ′f -> y   apply f until its result ≣ its argument (Jelly's ÐL):
# y = f(f(…f(x)…)) with f(y) ≣ y — the LAST result is returned, the one that
# matched the value before it.  f always runs at least once.  It never stops
# if f has no fixpoint from x.  Each step is a call of f plus one ≣, so a
# rewrite that removes one pair per pass (Jelly's Valid Parentheses: delete
# every "()" until nothing changes; Islands' flood fill one ring per pass) is
# O(n²) on an input of size n: n/2 passes over a list of up to n.
#
# Freeing.  Each intermediate value that ⍣ itself got from f (never x, never
# the result) is given back with ⌫ as soon as the next value is computed and
# compared — so a long run holds two lists, not all of them — EXCEPT when it
# is not a list, or it is the very block f returned (f may return its input;
# that is then ≣ itself and ⍣ stops), or f's result is a list that holds it as
# an element (‹_12a6_drop›; ∊, raw equality).  So f must not free its argument
# (⍣ does that), keep it in a variable, or nest it deeper than one level in
# its result.  An f that changes its argument IN PLACE and returns it stops
